I would like to start with specifying that most of the functions used for
the double list and the heap are taken and modified from the laboratory or copied
and modified between themselves.

First, the "main" function is divided into 3 parts: initialization, command reading and
deallocation. I will present the rest of the operations in groups, since it makes it
easier to explain.


1. ADD and ADDKW

The difference between these functions is made at the beginning, which I will explain separately.

Starting with ADD, it reads the ID, score, and keywords. If the ID already exists in the
list, prints "EXISTS" and frees the data that was read.
Otherwise it adds the file at the end of the double linked list.

On the other hand, ADDKW goes through the linked list to search for the file taken as input.
If it finds it, it reallocates the space used by keywords to make room for another one.

At the end, both of them make use of "add_keyword_to_tree" and "add_refrence".
(Apparently, I misspelled reference the entire time  in the code
and only realized because it was highlighted with red in this README) 
 
1.1 add_keyword_to_tree

add_keyword_to_tree is a recursive function that builds the tree. It searches for the first
letter of the keyword on the existing level of the tree, stopping if it reaches the end
or would destroy the alphabetical order. If it ends up stopping, it creates 
a cell downwards in the tree (a child) and passes the rest of the keyword as 
the argument for the node created (ex: if the word was "algebra" it would 
pass down "lgebra", then "gebra" and so on).
When the remaining string is the null terminator, it ends.


1.2 

add_refrence is also a recursive function that searches for a letter in the tree, given
the position of the letter in the word (the level in the tree). After checking if the
word is completed, it inserts the reference to the file in the list of the cell.
The insertion function is mostly the same as for any other linked list, with
the only addition that it does it depending on the alphabetical order of the file names.



2. DEL and DELKW 

Similar to the previous group, these operations rely on 2 functions: delete_refrence and
remove_empty_branches. Both of them are recursive, delete_refrence searches the tree,
takes the list and removes the reference passed as a parameter from it and remove_empty_branches
is pretty self explanatory, it removes all parts of the tree that are not useful, using
the conditions recommended in the document.

DEL:
Searches the list for the given ID. If it's not there, prints NOT FOUND. Otherwise, calls
delete_refrence and remove_empty_branches for every keyword.

DELKW:
Searches the list for the file. If it doesn't exist, prints NOT FOUND. If the keyword
isn't in the file's list, it prints OK without changes. If it is found, calls 
delete_refrence and remove_empty_branches for that specific keyword.



3. FIND and TOPK


FIND searches the tree for the characters of the keyword one by one. If it reaches
a terminal node for that exact keyword, it prints the count of references and
their file IDs (already in lexicographic order). If the keyword isn't in the tree,
it prints EMPTY.


TOPK works the same as FIND while getting to the terminal node. Then all the references
at that node are inserted into a max-heap that compares first by score and then by alphabetical
order. The first k elements are extracted from the heap and printed. If there are fewer
than k results, all of them are printed. If there are none, EMPTY.

All the heap functions used for TOPK are exactly the same as the ones I made in the
laboratory, with the only thing changing being that I transformed the integers into
references, and made another comparison function for the heap.



4. PRINT

Does a DFS traversal of the tree, going down first and then right at each level. The current
keyword is built character by character in a buffer as the traversal goes deeper.
Whenever a node with a non-empty reference list is reached, the accumulated keyword,
the number of associated files and their IDs are printed. Because the siblings are kept in
alphabetical order and the traversal always goes down before going right,
the output naturally comes out in alphabetical order.


5. PREFIX (bonus)

PREFIX is just reusing code from the other operations. The code from "FIND prefix" gets
the location of the prefix in the tree. Using that, with the algorithm from PRINT,
I make a new list of all the references to the files , duplicates getting avoided
by keeping the collected list sorted by ID and skipping references that are
already in it. At the end it prints the count and the IDs.




I think I managed to explain all of the operations. As in the last homework, I also made
my own operations, "files" and "tree". "files" printed the double linked list to standard
output (so I could see them even if I got a seg fault). "tree" was a modified version
of the function to print the tree given in the lab, letting me see all the nodes of the
tree, even the ones I failed to remove when needed. It also printed all the references
to the given words so was extremely similar to PRINT. I removed them since they were not
necessary but I still have them, saved.

